take now americans are still buying up a  storm even with the fastest inflation in  decades the latest government data  showed that the value of retail  purchases increased 1.7 in october that  is the biggest gain since march and it  represents a pickup from the eight  tenths of one percent increase seen the  previous month elevated savings and  rising wages for many households are  supporting the spending  how much should we read into this data  point and what does it signal for the  holiday shopping season let's ask angie  solonki she is national director of  retail sales for colliers angie always  great to speak with you on quick take  when it comes to this latest retail  sales report what kind of story do you  think the numbers tell us  well it's really interesting as you just  share inflation definitely is playing a  role but i would say that it's playing a  role in certain segments for example we  already know gas is going up but we're  also seeing a continued increase in  grocery spend so inflation has played a  role in that point where i think it's  representing around 64  of uh the the numbers hence the  complementary growth in that sector and  some of the beneficiaries from that are  going to be walmart because people are  being somewhat cautious as they look at  the upcoming holidays where they do want  to spend uh quite a bit of money as you  just shared buying just stuff and gifts  um purely because they can with the  elevated savings but also because people  have been vaccinated some have received  their booster shots and so they're  really looking to enjoy and have a good  time this holiday season  so  yeah when you look at the walmart  conversation it's very interesting that  i think what we're going to see is a  little bit of that inflation trickling  in but closer to maybe q2 of 2022.  that's really interesting given that  walmart shares today are down almost 3  percent because uh the company signaled  this bracing for more pressure from all  the  contagion caused uh snafus when it comes  to transportation and shipping logistics  and the costs overall are rising faster  than its prices because as a low-cost  retailer walmart only has so much room  to increase prices before it's it's  customer-based box at that  i want to dig in a little bit more on  that inflation figure because you said  uh that we're looking at about  two-thirds of the  increase that we saw in retail sales  attributed to inflation  how are retailers thinking about that  proportion do they see that uh staying  stable over the next say three months  six months or do they see inflation uh  representing a bigger chunk of their  sales in coming months  i would say scarlett what will be really  interesting is of course the holiday  season is probably the most important  season for retailers that's where you're  going to see the majority of the  holidays spend or consumers spend and so  what we've seen is with a lot of the  retailers who are well prepared due to  supply chain issues have stocked their  stores and stocked their shelves with  merchandise that they can sell at full  price with that that's going to help  with margins and hopefully offset some  of the concerns around inflation  therefore we're really forecasting a  pretty healthy 2022 year end  but it's going to be interesting to see  what happens  as we move into q1 and q2 of next year  i wonder though how consumers feel  because there's this narrative that  even though inflation is picking up and  consumer sentiment is souring because of  that pickup in inflation americans are  actually in decent financial shape they  have elevated savings they are making  more in wages overall  do retailers see that and it's a matter  of convincing people to spend even  though prices are higher or are they  adjusting for people's sentiment that  seems to signal they're very weary  they're concerned that things are going  the wrong direction  yeah not as of yet so we're still seeing  a very optimistic outlook from consumers  spend and therefore people are still  looking at the holidays as another way  to start sharing buying gifts we  actually saw you know most consumers  start their holiday shopping spree  as early as september of course in  october a lot of it had to do with  supply chain concerns i mean there's  been several interesting tick tock  messages out there from various um  consumers and patrons and also shop  owners and so you know kind of  challenging the supply chain but people  are still quite optimistic they i think  they're really tired of hearing about  you know the challenges from last year  they're looking forward to really  celebrating 2021 so we actually saw an  increase for example in apparel um and  you know we're thinking to ourselves wow  we went from athleisure wear to now you  know people buying more winter wear  buying you know shoes and dresses and  things that are going to be fun and  exciting for your end and it was roughly  around let's say a 23 increase in  apparel sales for the month of october  so when you start to see these numbers  you start to see people are are spending  now  will it start to rain in i think so but  i don't think it's going to occur this  year  interesting it just speaks to the the  power of retail therapy um on our  national psyche what product areas do  you see as being the most negatively  affected by all the inventory issues  that we do face at the end of this year  during the holiday shopping season the  the thick of the holiday shopping season  and into  2022 sure you know what was really  fascinating to me as a kind of secret  shopper myself is you know as i started  to kind of explore the different stores  and different categories what i found  very interesting to me was there were  definitely areas within like in the  grocery segment uh where they were  completely out of certain items and so i  was surprised by that um you know for  example just even water i was i thought  for you know at least we would have you  know no issues as it relates to water  but i saw a kind of a decline in that in  that area also some of the staple goods  um which was really surprising to me and  it just was again had to do with supply  chain just the merchandise didn't get  off the ship and into the stores fast  enough the other areas that we're also  seeing which i find very interesting is  that when you go into a store now yes it  is fully merchandise in stock but you  might start to see  a um you know less number of skus so not  as many as you know call it 100 skus in  a store it might be 50 skus in a store  but it's somewhat i wouldn't say it's an  illusion um but when you're going into a  store it still feels full and full of  merchandise but maybe the variety is  going to be less  right and you're seeing that balance  yeah when you refer to sku of course  we're talking about basically the the  selection of products that are available  out there um  i want to get from you a sense of what  the shortage of workers means for the  different retailers that are out there  obviously they've been trying to hire  for months now and it just becomes that  much more dire during the holiday  shopping season do you see them adapting  to  technological solutions and is that a  temporary fix or permanent fix  no definitely innovation uh as it  relates to technology in stores  is uh is in the forefront many retailers  have figured out how to  improve the overall um issue regarding  labor and uh whereby they put in if you  probably have noticed in many of the  larger format stores um you know the  contactless kind of the  point of sale so basically you're not  going through  a standard cashier you're basically  checking yourself out um and so they're  making they're adding more and more of  those uh checkout stations for for  consumers people are having a lot of fun  doing it it just kind of brings you back  uh and playing those kind of childhood  games of a grocery store  but that's where 